      "text": "THE AORIST",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 6,
      "bbox": [
        408,
        187,
        442,
        908
      ],
      "margin_number": 128,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "In this section you meet another new tense: the aorist. The aorist and the imperfect are the tenses most commonly used to denote past actions in Greek.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": 1,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "box",
      "order": 7,
      "bbox": [
        457,
        83,
        544,
        908
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "Whereas the imperfect describes a continuous or repeated past action (a process), the aorist describes the action as a single past event. Usually, a verb in the aorist is best translated ‘I –ed’, ‘you –ed’, etc., e.g. ἔπαυσα, ‘I stopped’, ἔθυσας, ‘you sacrificed’, and so on.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": "arrow",
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "order": 8,
      "bbox": [
        565,
        187,
        579,
        617
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "First and second aorists: English and Greek",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 9,
      "bbox": [
        588,
        187,
        640,
        908
      ],
      "margin_number": 129,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "In English there are two ways in which verbs may be put into the ‘past simple’ (the tense which is the nearest equivalent in English to the Greek aorist):",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": 2,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "box",
      "order": 10,
      "bbox": [
        655,
        83,
        737,
        908
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "• The majority of verbs add ‘-ed’ or ‘-d’ to the present stem, e.g. ‘I watch’ → ‘I watched’, ‘you like’ → ‘you liked’; but\n• some have a different stem in the past, e.g. ‘I sing’ → ‘I sang’, ‘they go’ → ‘they went’.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": "bullet",
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 11,
      "bbox": [
        762,
        238,
        775,
        532
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "The situation is similar in Greek:",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": 3,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "box",
      "order": 12,
      "bbox": [
        790,
        83,
        915,
        908
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "• The majority of verbs have a ‘first’ aorist – also called the ‘weak aorist’ – a form which is closely based on the present stem: it is this type of aorist you will meet here.\n• Some verbs have a less predictable ‘second aorist’ – also called ‘strong aorist’ – involving a strong change of stem: this type of aorist will be dealt with in section 6 C–D.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": "bullet",
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    }
